1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
A disease affects one person in a hundred.

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:04,494
Here are a thousand people.

3
00:00:04,494 --> 00:00:05,308
Ten of them are ill.

4
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:11,772
A test catches ninety nine per cent of the ill: all ten, near enough, test positive.

5
00:00:11,772 --> 00:00:17,963
But it also wrongly flags two per cent of the healthy people: about twenty of the nine hundred and ninety.

6
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:19,627
You test positive.

7
00:00:19,627 --> 00:00:21,281
What is the chance you are ill?

8
00:00:21,281 --> 00:00:23,234
Not ninety nine per cent.

9
00:00:23,234 --> 00:00:27,635
Of the thirty positives, only ten are ill: one in three.

10
00:00:27,635 --> 00:00:32,858
Turning probabilities round like this is Bayes' theorem, and this unit builds up to it.

11
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:42,983
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 11: Probability.

12
00:00:45,367 --> 00:00:46,436
Four parts.

13
00:00:46,436 --> 00:00:49,340
Dependent events and tree diagrams.

14
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:52,296
Conditional probability and independence.

15
00:00:52,296 --> 00:00:55,147
Bayes' theorem, for reasoning backwards.

16
00:00:55,147 --> 00:00:58,409
And repeated trials: the binomial distribution.

17
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:05,659
Draw one ball, keep it out, then draw another.

18
00:01:05,659 --> 00:01:12,365
The second draw depends on the first: if the first was red, only four reds remain among seven.

19
00:01:12,365 --> 00:01:16,428
These are dependent events, and a tree diagram keeps track of them.

20
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:18,965
Multiply along each path.

21
00:01:18,965 --> 00:01:23,796
Both red: five eighths times four sevenths, twenty fifty sixths.

22
00:01:23,796 --> 00:01:33,041
That is the multiplication theorem: the probability of A and B is the probability of A, times the probability of B given A.

23
00:01:33,041 --> 00:01:34,893
The four paths add up to one.

24
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:39,750
One of each colour: add the two middle paths, fifteen twenty eighths.

25
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:43,841
At least one black: one minus both red, nine fourteenths.

26
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:46,800
Cards, without replacement.

27
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:53,503
Four kings in fifty two; then three kings in the fifty one left: one in two twenty one.

28
00:01:53,733 --> 00:01:57,865
A king then a queen: all four queens are still there among fifty one.

29
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:01,751
Three aces: chain the multiplication three times.

30
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:08,962
Conditional probability.

31
00:02:08,962 --> 00:02:11,306
Suppose you are told that B has happened.

32
00:02:11,306 --> 00:02:17,504
Everything outside B is now impossible: B becomes the whole sample space.

33
00:02:17,504 --> 00:02:21,888
The chance of A is now the part of A inside B, as a fraction of B.

34
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:30,878
If knowing B changes nothing, so the probability of A given B is just the probability of A, the events are independent.

35
00:02:30,878 --> 00:02:35,290
Equivalently, the probability of both is the product of the two.

36
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:40,746
A Kathmandu college: a hundred students, sixty boys.

37
00:02:40,746 --> 00:02:44,210
Forty five boys and thirty five girls passed.

38
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:47,270
Put it in a two way table first, always.

39
00:02:47,270 --> 00:02:50,948
The chance a random student passed is eighty out of a hundred.

40
00:02:51,167 --> 00:02:54,645
Given a girl: work inside the girls' row only.

41
00:02:54,645 --> 00:02:57,926
Thirty five of forty passed: seven eighths.

42
00:02:57,926 --> 00:03:01,833
Given a pass: now the pass column is the whole world.

43
00:03:01,833 --> 00:03:05,557
Thirty five of the eighty: seven sixteenths.

44
00:03:05,557 --> 00:03:09,418
Same thirty five, different denominators, different questions.

45
00:03:10,633 --> 00:03:13,239
Two dice, thirty six outcomes.

46
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:17,640
Given that the sum is six: only five squares remain.

47
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:20,301
Three of them are both odd: three fifths.

48
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:21,511
Turn it round.

49
00:03:21,511 --> 00:03:24,311
Given both odd: nine squares.

50
00:03:24,311 --> 00:03:27,423
The same three have sum six: one third.

51
00:03:27,423 --> 00:03:31,565
Same favourable outcomes, different conditions, different answers.

52
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:33,827
Independent events.

53
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:38,071
'At least one solves it' is one minus 'all three fail'.

54
00:03:38,071 --> 00:03:41,222
Multiply the failure chances: a quarter.

55
00:03:41,222 --> 00:03:42,525
So three quarters.

56
00:03:42,733 --> 00:03:48,451
Exactly one: three separate cases, one solver and two failures each.

57
00:03:48,451 --> 00:03:50,651
Add them: eleven twenty fourths.

58
00:03:52,667 --> 00:03:54,557
Two classic confusions.

59
00:03:54,557 --> 00:03:57,291
A given B is not B given A.

60
00:03:57,291 --> 00:04:06,735
And independent is not the same as mutually exclusive: exclusive events cannot both happen, so they are about as dependent as events can be.

61
00:04:11,533 --> 00:04:13,865
Bayes' theorem reasons backwards.

62
00:04:13,865 --> 00:04:23,800
Several causes, E one and E two, each can produce an effect W. Going forwards along the tree, multiply along the paths.

63
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:28,128
The total probability of W is the sum of the paths that end in W.

64
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:29,949
Now W has happened.

65
00:04:29,949 --> 00:04:31,186
Which cause was it?

66
00:04:31,186 --> 00:04:38,022
The probability of E one given W is the E one path's share of all the W paths.

67
00:04:38,022 --> 00:04:39,004
That is Bayes' theorem.

68
00:04:40,233 --> 00:04:40,984
Two bags.

69
00:04:41,467 --> 00:04:47,395
Total probability: half times three fifths, plus half times a third: seven fifteenths.

70
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:55,050
Bayes: the bag A path, three tenths, as a share of seven fifteenths: nine fourteenths.

71
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:57,840
Sensible: bag A is the whiter bag.

72
00:04:59,867 --> 00:05:00,523
A factory.

73
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:05,235
Total probability of a defective bulb: point zero two four.

74
00:05:05,467 --> 00:05:10,026
The A path is point zero one two of it: exactly a half.

75
00:05:10,026 --> 00:05:14,188
A makes more bulbs, but makes them better, and the two effects cancel.

76
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:18,663
Now the cold open, done properly.

77
00:05:18,663 --> 00:05:24,665
One per cent ill, a ninety nine per cent catch rate, a two per cent false alarm rate.

78
00:05:24,665 --> 00:05:29,301
Total probability of a positive: point zero two nine seven.

79
00:05:29,301 --> 00:05:32,308
The share from the ill: exactly one third.

80
00:05:32,308 --> 00:05:36,688
That is why a positive screening result is always checked with a second test.

81
00:05:41,467 --> 00:05:47,758
Toss a coin, roll a die, again and again, the same chance of success each time.

82
00:05:47,758 --> 00:05:59,502
The probability of exactly r successes in n trials: choose which r trials succeed, n C r ways, each with probability p to the r times q to the n minus r.

83
00:05:59,502 --> 00:06:04,455
The mean number of successes is n p, and the variance n p q.

84
00:06:04,667 --> 00:06:07,794
Here are the probabilities for five trials.

85
00:06:07,794 --> 00:06:13,327
With a fair coin, p a half, the bars are symmetric about the mean, two and a half.

86
00:06:13,327 --> 00:06:20,849
Make success rarer, like rolling a six, and the bars pile up near zero; make it likely, and they slide right.

87
00:06:22,067 --> 00:06:22,577
Coins.

88
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:30,352
Exactly three: five C three is ten, times a half to the fifth: five sixteenths.

89
00:06:30,567 --> 00:06:38,038
At least three: ten plus five plus one, over thirty two: a half, as symmetry demands.

90
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:40,327
Mean: two and a half heads.

91
00:06:42,333 --> 00:06:42,754
A die.

92
00:06:43,567 --> 00:06:53,023
Exactly two sixes: six ways, times one thirty sixth, times twenty five thirty sixths: twenty five over two sixteen.

93
00:06:53,233 --> 00:06:57,084
At least one: one minus no sixes at all: just over a half.

94
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:00,047
Mean two thirds; variance five ninths.

95
00:07:02,667 --> 00:07:04,088
Exam tip.

96
00:07:04,088 --> 00:07:06,744
At least one is one minus none.

97
00:07:06,744 --> 00:07:10,060
Write down n, p and q before anything else.

98
00:07:12,033 --> 00:07:14,097
Pause and think about the thousand people.

99
00:07:20,967 --> 00:07:25,388
About a third: ten true positives against about twenty false ones.

100
00:07:27,367 --> 00:07:27,802
One more.

101
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:39,002
Point two: for independent events, multiply.

102
00:07:39,002 --> 00:07:41,129
Zero would mean mutually exclusive.

103
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:44,369
Quick recap.

104
00:07:44,369 --> 00:07:47,664
Multiply along a tree for dependent events.

105
00:07:47,664 --> 00:07:50,606
Conditioning shrinks the sample space.

106
00:07:50,606 --> 00:07:52,989
Independent events multiply.

107
00:07:52,989 --> 00:07:56,114
Bayes' theorem finds the cause from the effect.

108
00:07:56,114 --> 00:08:03,179
And the binomial formula counts successes in repeated trials, with mean n p and variance n p q.

109
00:08:07,367 --> 00:08:08,723
That was probability.

110
00:08:08,723 --> 00:08:14,608
Next, derivatives: limits, rates of change and how to differentiate anything.

111
00:08:14,608 --> 00:08:15,146
See you there.

